Summary of important user-visible changes for l-uq
==================================================

l-uq 2.0.0
----------

 ** First release packaged for GNU Octave. The package installs with
    `pkg install l-uq-2.0.0.tar.gz` and needs no other packages: the
    Statistics toolbox / statistics package dependency has been removed
    throughout (see below).

 ** PDF_l, CDF_l and Random_l no longer call MATLAB's name-dispatching
    pdf/cdf/random. All ten families are now written out in closed form
    in the new luq_dist, on top of functions core to both MATLAB and
    Octave (erf, erfinv, gamma, gammainc, gammaincinv). Parameter
    layouts, location shifts and numerical results are unchanged.

 ** Random_l now generates variates by inverse transform sampling. The
    distribution sampled is unchanged, but the exact stream of variates
    differs from the previous random()-based implementation for a given
    seed.

 ** Parameter_estimation uses erfinv in place of norminv, and
    Identify_dist_bootstrap uses the new luq_percentile in place of
    prctile. luq_percentile follows numpy.percentile's linear
    interpolation convention rather than prctile's, so that the
    bootstrap intervals reported by the MATLAB, Octave and Python
    implementations agree.

 ** New KSStat: the Kolmogorov-Smirnov statistic between a fitted family
    and a raw sample. Unlike JSDiv it requires no binning, so it can
    confirm that a divergence comparison is not an artifact of the bins.

 ** parameter_identify is fixed. Identify_dist only ever returns the
    single closest family, so the previous implementation raised an
    index-out-of-bounds error for any K > 1, and for K = 1 passed a 1x1
    cell rather than a char to Parameter_estimation. It now walks the
    ratio-diagram ranking and returns the top K feasible candidates, in
    step with the Python implementation. The output arguments have
    changed: [Distribution_type, Parameter, D_sorted, L_sample, skipped].

 ** New demo_octave: a walkthrough that runs in both Octave and MATLAB
    with no extra packages, exercising lmom, Identify_dist, fit_best,
    parameter_identify, Identify_dist_bootstrap, PDF_l, CDF_l, Random_l,
    JSDiv, KLDiv and KSStat. The MATLAB-only demo_example, which
    compares against fitdist, is not part of the Octave package.

 ** JSDiv and KLDiv accept a fit and a raw sample directly --
    JSDiv(Name, Parameter, Data) -- and bin internally, instead of
    requiring the caller to construct matching histograms by hand. The
    two-vector form JSDiv(P, Q) is unchanged.
